[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m [1mSection 3 — The notification engine[0m

  This is the heart of it, and writing the rules out surfaced one simplification to Section 2.

  [1mKinds collapse from three to two.[0m [38;5;153mdue[39m (at [38;5;153mdueAt[39m) and [38;5;153moverdue[39m (just after [38;5;153mdueAt[39m) are the same moment described twice —
  nobody wants both. So: [38;5;153mdue-soon[39m, fired at [38;5;153mdueAt - leadTime[39m, and [38;5;153moverdue[39m, fired at [38;5;153mdueAt[39m. Only for tasks with a [38;5;153mdueAt[39m
  and no [38;5;153mcompletedAt[39m.

  [1mLevel-triggered, not edge-triggered.[0m The engine never asks "did this threshold pass during this tick?" — it asks
  "should this kind have fired by now, and hasn't it?" ([38;5;153mnow >= threshold && !alreadyFired[39m). This single choice is what
  makes the whole system robust: a tab closed for six hours, a sleeping laptop, a missed tick, a clock adjustment, and a
  fresh page load all take the identical code path. There's no separate catch-up routine to get wrong, because catch-up
  [3mis[0m the normal path.

  [1mStorm control.[0m The flip side is that opening the app after a week could produce forty notifications at once. Rule:
  catch-up events are always recorded in the notifications list, but only events newer than a staleness window (24
  hours) raise a toast. You get the history without forty OS popups.

  [1mSuppression.[0m If a task is already overdue the first time it's evaluated, its [38;5;153mdue-soon[39m is marked fired without
  notifying — being told something is "due in 15 minutes" when it was due yesterday is noise.

  [1mCadence.[0m The scheduler ticks every 30 seconds, plus on load, on [38;5;153mvisibilitychange[39m → visible, and after any task
  mutation. The engine is pure and linear in task count, so this is cheap.

  [1mTesting.[0m [38;5;153mnow[39m is a parameter, so every rule above is a table-driven unit test with no timers.

  Does the two-kind simplification and the staleness-window behavior look right?

